-- title:
--   Level automorphisms translate the ends of the blown-up chart
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ and $\ell$ are primes with $q\ge 5$, $\ell\ge 3$, $\ell\neq q$, and $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$ and $\ell\nmid M'$. The field $L$ has characteristic $0$, $\xi\in L$ is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity, and `hι` provides a ring homomorphism $\iota\colon L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$. The intermediate field $K$ of $L\subseteq\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ is pinned by `hK`: it is [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to the $q$-expansion function field [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79), that is, $K$ is generated over $L$ inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ by the coefficientwise images of that field of $q$-expansions over $\mathbb Q$; here `levelH (q*ℓ) M'` is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell)^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   The ring $A$ is a henselian discrete valuation domain with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with $[\mathrm{Algebra}\,A\,L]$ and $[\mathrm{IsFractionRing}\,A\,L]$, with $q\in\mathfrak m_A$ (`hAq`), with $\xi$ in the image of $A$ (`hξA`), and with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j\in K$ is nonzero and has Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the classical $j$-function read in $L$ (`hj`). The element $\varpi$ generates $\mathfrak m_A$ (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t\in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\cdot u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`). Write $C:=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and let `jChartFin` denote $j$ as an element of $C$. The ideal $y\subseteq C$ is maximal (`hy`), contains $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and is supersingular in the sense of `hss`: every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from $C$ to an algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ with kernel $y$ sends $j$ into [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of those $j$-invariants for which every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` is the Drinfeld-chart rigidity block for the two-chart integral model $\mathfrak X:=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) (the pushout of $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the two chart algebras). It requires, for every point $z$ of $\mathfrak X$ at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ (transported from $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$ along `toBase`) lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and for every point $y'$ of `XFin` $=\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ mapping to $z$ under `ιFin` whose ideal satisfies the same supersingularity condition `hss'`, the existence of a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a homomorphism $\sigma\colon A\to W$ with $\mathfrak m_W=(\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f,u,v\in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f\equiv$ [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18) $=X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S:=W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v-fu\bigr)$, subject to seven riders (summarised here, with $\mathrm{toC}$ the map to the completion, $\mathrm{mkS}$ the quotient map and $\mathrm{germY}$ the canonical map $C\to\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$): (i) $e$ carries the germ of each $a\in A$ to the class of $C(\sigma a)$; (ii) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, every automorphism $\tau$ of $K/L$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ` — that is, acting on quotients of weight-$k$ modular forms for $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions by the substitution `conjElemN (q*ℓ) γ⁻¹`, read through any $\iota$ with $\iota\xi=e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$ — preserves $C$; (iii) if moreover $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, the induced endomorphism of $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'$; (iv) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ as above inducing the identity modulo $y'$, there are $\theta\in\mathrm{Aut}(S)$, $c\in W$ and $M\in\mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ with $\theta$ compatible with $\tau$ on the image of $C$, fixing constants, with $\theta(X_{j})\equiv\sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i$ modulo the square of $(X_0,X_1)$, with $c^{q+1}\equiv 1$ and $M_{ij}\equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, with $c\equiv 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, and $c\not\equiv 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; (v) two primes of $S$ containing $C(\sigma\varpi)$ but not both $X_0,X_1$, carrying linear forms $a_iX_0+b_iX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^2$ with $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$, have distinct contractions to the stalk; (vi) for a prime $P$ of $S$ of this kind whose linear form is $X_0$, an element $a\in C$ has $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a)$ in the contraction of $P$ precisely when every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in $\mathfrak m_A$; (vii) the Igusa-centre rider: [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0\in A$ with that element congruent to $a_0$ modulo $y'$, an integer $e_0\ge 1$ and $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form is a unit at every pair $(a,b)\in W^2$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb-ab^q\in\mathfrak m_W$, such that $e$ sends the class of that element minus $a_0$ to $\mathrm{mkS}\,h$.
--
--   A specific point is then fixed: $z$ together with $\varpi_z$, `hϖz`, `hz`, a point $y'$ of `XFin` over $z$ with `hy'`, its supersingularity `hss'`, and `hy'y`: $y'$ corresponds to $y$. A single chart witness at $z$ is fixed as data: $W_1,\sigma_1,f_1,u_1,v_1$ with $u_1,v_1$ units (`hu₁`, `hv₁`), $f_1$ congruent to the Drinfeld form modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ from the completed stalk onto $S_1:=W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{q+1}))v_1-f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for this witness exactly the seven riders (i)–(vii) listed above.
--
--   The ideal $J\subseteq C$ is pinned by `hJ` as the infimum of all ideals of the form $\rho_\tau^{-1}\bigl((e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY})^{-1}(\mathrm{mkS}(C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)),\mathrm{mkS}\,X_0,\mathrm{mkS}\,X_1)\bigr)$, where $\gamma$ runs over $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, $\tau$ over the level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, and $\rho_\tau$ is the induced endomorphism of $C$. The subalgebra $B$ is pinned by `hB` as the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ obtained by adjoining to $C$ the set $\{x\in K:\ x\varpi_t\in J\}$, that is $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$. The valuation subring $W\subseteq K$ contains $B$ (`hBW`). Further hypotheses on these data: `hR1` ($C\le B$, and every element of $K$ is a quotient $g/h$ with $g,h\in B$, $h\neq 0$); `hR2` ($A\to B$ formally smooth and of finite presentation, and $B/\varpi B$ of Krull dimension at most $1$); `hR3`, five clauses, namely that an element of $L$ lies in $W$ exactly when it comes from $A$, that $\mathfrak m_W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that for $b\in C$ one has $b\in y$ exactly when $b$ lies in $W$ with image in $\mathfrak m_W$, and that $f\in W$ exactly when $f=g/h$ with $g,h\in B$ and $h$ a unit of $W$; and `hEQ`, five clauses: (1) for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho$ from $B$ onto [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) whose vanishing locus is the set of $b\in B$ with image in $\mathfrak m_W$, compatible with $A\to\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, and equivariant for level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W$, through an element $c\in\mathbb F_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma,c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting by `hAction`, with $c\neq 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; (2) level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, preserve $B$; (3) every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is hit, via some $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$, by the image under $\tau$ of all $b\in B$ with image in $\mathfrak m_W$; (4) an element of $B$ all of whose such translates have image in $\mathfrak m_W$ is divisible by $\varpi$ in $B$; (5) a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ that preserves $y$ inside $C$ preserves $W$. Finally, $O\subseteq K$ is a subring satisfying `hO`: there are $a\in J$ with nonzero image in $K$, and, with $B_a$ the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ obtained by adjoining to $C$ the set $\{x\in K:\ \exists i\in J,\ xa=i\}$, a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a$ such that $f\in O$ exactly when $f=g/h$ with $g,h\in B_a$ and $h\notin P$, such that every $b\in y$ whose image lies in $O$ is a non-unit of $O$, and such that $B$ is not contained in $O$. The last data are $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, an automorphism $\tau$ of $K/L$ with `IsLevelAutAt` at $\gamma^{-1}$ (`hτ`), and `hτW`: $f\in W$ if and only if $\tau f\in W$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of a subring $O'\subseteq K$ with three properties.
--
--   First, $O'$ satisfies the same description as $O$: there exist $a\in J$ with nonzero image in $K$ and a maximal ideal $P$ of the corresponding $B_a=C[\{x: xa\in J\}]$ such that $f\in O'$ exactly when $f=g/h$ with $g,h\in B_a$, $h\notin P$; every $b\in y$ whose image lies in $O'$ is a non-unit of $O'$; and $B$ is not contained in $O'$.
--
--   Second, for every $f\in K$: $f\in O$ if and only if $\tau f\in O'$.
--
--   Third, for all prime ideals $\mathfrak p,\mathfrak p'$ of $C$ such that each contains $\varpi$ and satisfies $\mathfrak p\subseteq y$, $\mathfrak p\neq y$ (respectively $\mathfrak p'\subseteq y$, $\mathfrak p'\neq y$): if there is a prime $\mathfrak q$ of $O$ which contains the image of $\varpi$ whenever that image lies in $O$, for which some $b\in y$ with image in $O$ lies outside $\mathfrak q$, and which contracts to $\mathfrak p$ in the sense that for all $c\in C$ with image in $O$ one has $c\in\mathfrak p$ iff that image lies in $\mathfrak q$; and if $\tau$ matches $\mathfrak p$ with $\mathfrak p'$ in the sense that for all $c\in C$ with $\tau c\in C$ one has $c\in\mathfrak p$ iff $\tau c\in\mathfrak p'$; then there is a prime $\mathfrak q$ of $O'$ with the three corresponding properties: it contains the image of $\varpi$ whenever that lies in $O'$, some $b\in y$ with image in $O'$ lies outside it, and for all $c\in C$ with image in $O'$ one has $c\in\mathfrak p'$ iff that image lies in $\mathfrak q$.
--
--   This is the transport step for the reduction geometry at a supersingular point of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$: a level automorphism preserving the exceptional valuation subring $W$ carries each end of the weighted blow-up $C[J/\varpi_t]$ of the supersingular chart to another such end, together with the primes below $y$ (the Igusa branches) that it sees. It is used in the subsequent counting of the set of ends of the blown-up chart and its identification as a localisation.
